{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"raw":false,"start_index":0,"url":"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/fr/document/EPRS_BRI(2022)729375"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/fr/document/EPRS_BRI(2022)729375:\nWhile the UN Human Development Index, which assesses progress in the standard of living, health and education, ranks Russia among the 'very high human development' countries, other data suggest a more nuanced reality. Russia has gone from Soviet-era egalitarianism to extremes of wealth and poverty. Economic growth during the first decade under Vladimir Putin's leadership slightly reduced the gap between rich and poor, but inequality is still higher than in most developed countries. Factors such as corruption and low taxes for the rich exacerbate the income gap. Free public healthcare and a relatively inclusive education system apparently mitigate inequality. However, many hospitals are ill-equipped, and universities struggle to compare with foreign counterparts. Since 2014 and the invasion of Crimea, Russians' living conditions have deteriorated. Russia's war of aggression on Ukraine and the imposition of Western sanctions is already placing a burden on the poorest members of Russian society, as prices increase and staple goods begin to run out. The social contract between Russians and Putin, often considered as one in which citizens ceded certain freedoms and civil liberties in exchange for stability and prosperity, is now under strain. This briefing is partly based on and updates previous EPRS publications on socioeconomic inequality and on the education system."}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"# Review round 1 continuation — Vladimir Putin, Russia, 2023\n\nThe continuation pass used the existing evidence register and searched only unresolved themes. It added evidence across all eight chapters without assigning scores.\n\nThe requested notebook and manifest still could not be written: filesystem tool execution failed before the command ran with `bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted`. The additions and manifest changes below are therefore the authoritative persistence handoff.\n\n## Continuation search record\n\n### 1B candidate pool\n\nQueries included:\n\n- `Putin 2023 Belarus tactical nuclear weapons CTBT nuclear rhetoric official transcript`\n- `site:un.org Putin 2023 nuclear threats Belarus New START CTBT`\n- `Russia 2023 nuclear risk crisis communications United States deconfliction`\n- `Путин 2023 ядерное оружие Беларусь ДВЗЯИ заявление`\n\nOpened source families:\n\n- SIPRI\n- European Union delegation to the UN\n- Current Time, linking the Kremlin primary transcript\n- NTI\n- US State Department\n- Turkish/European media candidates\n\n### 2B candidate pool\n\nQueries included:\n\n- `Russia Ukraine negotiations 2023 Putin ceasefire peace talks primary statement`\n- `UN OHCHR 2023 Russian prisoners Ukraine torture accountability command`\n- `Putin 2023 war aims speech Ukraine official Kremlin transcript`\n- `Russia Wagner Africa 2023 abuses proxy control Putin report`\n\nOpened:\n\n- UN Geneva/OHCHR\n- OHCHR detention report index\n- US State Department Wagner candidate\n\n### 3B–4B candidate pool\n\nQueries included:\n\n- `Russia 2023 regional elections opposition candidates Golos election observation law`\n- searches for Golos, regional election manipulation, candidate registration, and election-observer detention\n- continuing HRW/Freedom House corroboration leads\n\nOpened:\n\n- Stockholm Centre for Eastern European Studies\n- European Council on Foreign Relations\n- Transparencia Electoral/Golos\n\n### 5B–6B candidate pool\n\nQueries included:\n\n- `Russia 2023 GDP growth inflation wages poverty EBRD Bank Russia`\n- `Russia 2023 healthcare education pensions poverty social outcomes`\n- source-specific searches for EBRD, OSW, World Bank, European Parliament, and macroeconomic reporting\n\nOpened:\n\n- Voice of America economic analysis\n- OSW public-services analysis\n- EBRD PDF candidate\n- European Parliament Research Service background\n\n### 7B candidate pool\n\nQueries included:\n\n- `Putin 2023 corruption family wealth investigation Navalny retaliation`\n- searches for beneficial ownership, family enrichment, Navalny/ACF investigations, asset declarations, and inquiry retaliation\n\nThe pool largely returned older allegations, post-period retrospectives, videos, social posts, or materials lacking usable underlying financial/legal locators.\n\n### 8B candidate pool\n\nResearch reused the new economic, public-service, election, nuclear, and OHCHR findings, together with source-specific searches for:\n\n- primary strategy and budget choices;\n- war and defense mobilization;\n- electoral control;\n- public-service implementation;\n- Wagner-related crisis management.\n\n---\n\n# New and revised ledger items\n\n## E002 split into distinct source-claim units\n\nThe previous E002 bundled multiple materially distinct UN findings. Preserve E002 as superseded accounting context or change it to `context`, with the reason that its claims are now deterministically split into E012–E015.\n\n## E012 — Russian indiscriminate attack on Uman apartment building\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ukraine.un.org/en/250021-un-commission-inquiry-ukraine-finds-continued-war-crimes-and-human-rights-violations-gravely#uman|Russian-attack-Uman-killed-24-civilians`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Title:** “UN Commission of Inquiry on Ukraine finds continued war crimes and human rights violations”\n- **Publisher:** United Nations in Ukraine / Independent International Commission of Inquiry\n- **Date:** 20 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Additional indiscriminate attacks…” and the Uman example.\n- **Claim:** The Commission investigated a Russian attack on a multistorey apartment building in Uman in April 2023 that killed 24 civilians, mostly women and children, and rendered part of the building uninhabitable.\n- **Excerpt:** “24 civilians, mostly women and children, were killed.”\n- **Source profile:** UN investigative commission; `high`; direct investigation and linked formal report.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 event.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Russian-force responsibility is established; this page does not prove Putin personally selected or ordered the attack.\n- **Contrary/limitations:** Incident-level command responsibility remains unresolved.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.9, 2B.10.\n\n## E013 — Widespread and systematic torture by Russian authorities\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ukraine.un.org/en/250021-un-commission-inquiry-ukraine-finds-continued-war-crimes-and-human-rights-violations-gravely#torture|Russian-authorities-widespread-systematic-torture`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “The investigations confirmed…”\n- **Claim:** The Commission confirmed that Russian authorities used torture in a widespread and systematic way in different detention facilities, including in areas under Russian control in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia.\n- **Source profile:** UN investigative commission; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** Evidence collected and reported in 2023, with continuity from the invasion period.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Strong state-authority nexus, but no personal Putin order is identified.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9; 3B only if the formatter clearly marks occupied-territory/international-conflict scope rather than domestic Russian safety.\n\n## E014 — Sexual violence and torture by Russian personnel\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ukraine.un.org/en/250021-un-commission-inquiry-ukraine-finds-continued-war-crimes-and-human-rights-violations-gravely#sexual-violence|Russian-personnel-rape-and-torture`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “The Commission’s recent investigations…”\n- **Claim:** The Commission found that rape and other sexual violence by Russian personnel were often accompanied by beatings, strangulation, suffocation, cutting, shooting near victims, or killing.\n- **Source profile:** UN investigative commission; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** Target-period investigation and publication.\n- **Ruler attribution:** State-force pattern; no direct personal-order evidence.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9.\n\n## E015 — Unlawful deportation of Ukrainian children\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ukraine.un.org/en/250021-un-commission-inquiry-ukraine-finds-continued-war-crimes-and-human-rights-violations-gravely#children|Russian-authorities-unlawful-child-deportation`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “The Commission investigated further accounts…”\n- **Claim:** The Commission documented the transfer of 31 children to Russia in May 2022 and concluded that it was unlawful deportation constituting a war crime; its 2023 report expressed concern about measures apparently intended to keep some transferred children in Russia for prolonged periods.\n- **Source profile:** UN investigative commission; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** The transfer occurred in 2022, but the continued-retention concern and formal finding were documented in 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Russian-authority policy context; personal Putin attribution not established on this page.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9; 3B.7 only as cross-border vulnerable-group context, not domestic-safety evidence.\n\n---\n\n## E016 — Putin signaled possible CTBT reversal and testing\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.sipri.org/commentary/expert-comment/2023/russias-potential-withdrawal-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty-underlines-urgent-arms-control#opening|Putin-CTBT-testing-signal`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://www.sipri.org/commentary/expert-comment/2023/russias-potential-withdrawal-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty-underlines-urgent-arms-control\n- **Title:** “Russia’s potential withdrawal from the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty underlines urgent arms-control challenges”\n- **Publisher:** SIPRI\n- **Date:** 17 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Opening two paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** SIPRI reported that Putin’s 5 October Valdai statement hinted that Russia might revoke CTBT ratification and resume nuclear-explosive testing for the first time since the Soviet period.\n- **Main points:** The analysis interpreted the move primarily as geopolitical signaling rather than a technically necessary weapons-development measure.\n- **Source profile:** Independent arms-control research institute; `high` for technical and treaty context, though intent is analytical.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023 Putin statement and legislative process.\n- **Ruler attribution:** High for signaling; the Duma enacted the formal ratification reversal.\n- **Contrary/mitigating points:** SIPRI cautioned against overstating the technical effect and noted that the United States itself had not ratified the CTBT.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.10.\n\n## E017 — CTBT move weakened arms-control architecture but did not alter doctrine\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.sipri.org/commentary/expert-comment/2023/russias-potential-withdrawal-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty-underlines-urgent-arms-control#significance|CTBT-reversal-risk-and-limitations`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs beginning “The latest announcement…” and “Even with regard to arms control…”\n- **Claim:** SIPRI assessed the move as weakening international arms-control architecture and strategic stability, while cautioning that it did not itself change Russia’s nuclear doctrine, force posture, or incentives and that preparations at Novaya Zemlya did not prove an imminent explosive test.\n- **Source profile:** SIPRI; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 assessment.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Policy-level attribution to Putin’s announced direction.\n- **Contrary/mitigating role:** Important limitation against overstating immediate capability or testing consequences.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.3, 1B.4, 1B.8, 1B.10.\n\n## E018 — Putin-directed deployment of tactical nuclear weapons to Belarus\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.currenttime.tv/a/yadernoe-oruzhie-v-belarusi/32452140.html#opening|Putin-Belarus-tactical-nuclear-transfer`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://www.currenttime.tv/a/yadernoe-oruzhie-v-belarusi/32452140.html\n- **Title:** Russian-language report on Putin’s announcement of tactical-nuclear deployment to Belarus\n- **Publisher:** Current Time\n- **Date:** 9 June 2023\n- **Locator:** First four paragraphs; article links Kremlin event `http://kremlin.ru/events/president/news/71326`.\n- **Claim:** Putin said that storage preparations in Belarus would finish on 7–8 July and that Russia would then begin moving tactical nuclear weapons there.\n- **Excerpt:** “сразу же после этого российская сторона начнет перевозку… тактического ядерного вооружения” — Russia would begin transferring tactical nuclear arms immediately afterward.\n- **Additional factual detail:** Russian and Belarusian defense ministers signed a storage arrangement in May 2023; Shoigu said Moscow retained control and use authority.\n- **Source profile:** Reputable regional public-service media carrying and linking an official Kremlin statement; `medium_high`.\n- **Final-evidence use:** `final_evidence`\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 action.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Direct Putin announcement; use authority remained with Moscow.\n- **Contrary/mitigating points:** Russia stated that control was not transferred to Belarus. Actual weapon numbers and independent verification are not established by this article.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.3, 1B.5, 1B.6, 1B.8, 1B.10; 2B.3, 2B.5.\n\n## E019 — EU statement on Belarus deployment and prior commitments\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.eeas.europa.eu/delegations/un-new-york/[belarus-statement]#deployment|EU-assessment-Belarus-nuclear-deployment`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** The fetched Russian-language EEAS page titled “Заявление ЕС … объявлением России о размещении ядерного оружия…”\n- **Publisher:** European Union delegation to the United Nations\n- **Date:** 2023 Security Council briefing\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs beginning “ЕС осуждает заявление России…” through the discussion of the Budapest Memorandum and 2022 nuclear-power statement.\n- **Claim:** The EU condemned Russia’s 25 March announcement as part of continuing reckless nuclear rhetoric and an escalatory step connected to the war. It contrasted the deployment with Belarus’s earlier commitment to eliminate nuclear weapons from its territory and Russia’s January 2022 affirmation that nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.\n- **Source profile:** Intergovernmental diplomatic statement; `medium_high` for formal positions and quoted commitments, `medium` for evaluative language.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 response.\n- **Ruler attribution:** The announcement was Russia’s and was publicly made by Putin.\n- **Contrary/limitations:** Adversarial diplomatic assessment; use primarily for formal commitments and contemporary reaction, not as sole support for recklessness.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.4–1B.6, 1B.8, 1B.10.\n\n## E020 — NTI evidence of nuclear-capable systems delivered to Belarus\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.nti.org/countries/belarus/#missile|Russia-delivered-Iskander-Belarus-2023`\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n- **URL:** https://www.nti.org/countries/belarus/\n- **Title:** “Belarus”\n- **Publisher:** Nuclear Threat Initiative\n- **Date:** Updated before archive closure in April 2026\n- **Locator:** Missile section.\n- **Claim:** NTI records that Belarus received Russian Iskander nuclear-capable missile systems in 2023.\n- **Source profile:** Nuclear-security NGO; `medium_high` for technical country profiles.\n- **Period fit:** Directly concerns 2023, but the page is a later-maintained country profile.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Russian state transfer; Putin direction is supported by E018 rather than this item alone.\n- **Limitations:** Later compilation and no displayed underlying citation in fetched text.\n- **Mappings:** 1B.3, 1B.5, 1B.6, 1B.10.\n\n---\n\n## E021 — OHCHR prisoner and civilian-detainee findings\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1866/hrc-ohchr-report-on-ukraine-09-october-2023#torture|Russian-authorities-widespread-torture-POWs-civilians`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1866/hrc-ohchr-report-on-ukraine-09-october-2023\n- **Title:** “Ukraine: Russian forces implicated in ‘blatant and unabated violations’”\n- **Publisher:** UN Geneva / OHCHR\n- **Date:** 9 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs and transcript items 5–7.\n- **Claim:** OHCHR reported that torture and ill-treatment of civilians and prisoners of war by Russian authorities was widespread and included electric shocks, sexual violence, and severe beatings.\n- **Evidence basis:** 117 field visits, 27 detention-center inspections, 23 observed trials, and 1,226 interviews.\n- **Source profile:** UN human-rights monitoring body; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** Covers events through 31 July 2023 and earlier invasion-period detention.\n- **Ruler attribution:** State-authority responsibility established; personal Putin order not established.\n- **Contrary:** Russia’s UN representative rejected OHCHR’s methodology and conclusions.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9.\n\n## E022 — Russian authorities took no discernible accountability steps\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1866/hrc-ohchr-report-on-ukraine-09-october-2023#accountability|Russia-no-discernible-accountability-June-amnesty`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “The report also deplored…”\n- **Claim:** OHCHR stated that Russian authorities had taken no discernible steps to ensure accountability for violations by their security forces. It also noted a June 2023 law effectively granting amnesty to Russian service members for an overly broad range of crimes.\n- **Source profile:** UN monitoring body; `high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 legal and accountability record.\n- **Ruler attribution:** State-policy attribution; personal Putin signature or direction of the June law was not established from the fetched source and should not be assumed.\n- **Contrary:** Russia formally disputed the report.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.9; 3B.2 and 3B.4 only with clear international-conflict scope.\n\n## E023 — Russia withdrew from the Black Sea Grain Initiative\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1866/hrc-ohchr-report-on-ukraine-09-october-2023#grain|Russia-withdrawal-grain-initiative-attacks`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Devastating long-term effects…”, transcript item 11.\n- **Claim:** Following Russia’s withdrawal from the UN-brokered Black Sea Grain Initiative, OHCHR observed increased Russian attacks affecting grain-export infrastructure and surrounding civilian and historic buildings.\n- **Source profile:** UN human-rights monitoring report; `high` for chronology and monitored effects.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 decision and aftermath.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Russian government decision; personal Putin direction requires a primary presidential source if asserted.\n- **Contrary/limitations:** This source does not reconstruct negotiations preceding withdrawal.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.1, 2B.2, 2B.7, 2B.10; 5B.1 and 8B.9 as external-policy context.\n\n## E024 — OHCHR index establishes target-period detention reports\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/reports/detention#2023-reports|OHCHR-detention-report-scope`\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n- **URL:** https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/reports/detention\n- **Publisher:** UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine\n- **Locator:** Entries dated 24 March and 27 June 2023.\n- **Claim:** OHCHR issued a March 2023 report on POW treatment covering 24 February 2022–23 February 2023 and a June report on civilian detention covering 24 February 2022–23 May 2023.\n- **Source profile:** UN report index; `high` for report existence and scope.\n- **Limitations:** Gateway/index only for substantive claims; E021 and E022 provide the precise accepted claims.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.4, 2B.9 as report-scope context.\n\n---\n\n## E025 — Regional elections as controlled rehearsal for 2024\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://sceeus.se/en/publications/russias-2023-september-elections-a-dress-rehearsal-for-vladimir-putin/#executive-summary|2023-regional-elections-controlled-results`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://sceeus.se/en/publications/russias-2023-september-elections-a-dress-rehearsal-for-vladimir-putin/\n- **Title:** “Russia’s 2023 September Elections: A Dress Rehearsal for Vladimir Putin”\n- **Publisher:** Stockholm Centre for Eastern European Studies\n- **Date:** 27 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Executive Summary and first two body paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** The 8–10 September elections covered 41 regional contests and served as a test for the 2024 presidential election. The analysis described them as controlled and lacking genuine competitiveness, with United Russia prevailing almost everywhere.\n- **Source profile:** Government-funded foreign-policy research center; `medium_high` for expert analysis; references Golos, official results, UN, and media.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 elections.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Kremlin/regime-level; direct Putin action is clearer through his endorsement of electronic voting in E029.\n- **Contrary:** Khakassia retained a Communist governor, and limited local opposition success occurred.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.8, 4B.10; 8B.1, 8B.3, 8B.6, 8B.9.\n\n## E026 — Electronic voting reduced transparency\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://sceeus.se/en/publications/russias-2023-september-elections-a-dress-rehearsal-for-vladimir-putin/#electronic-voting|electronic-voting-black-box`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Second, the elections marked…”\n- **Claim:** Nearly four million votes were cast electronically. The system operated as a “black box” to independent observers, reducing transparency and making fraud harder to document. Public employees and economically dependent voters were strongly encouraged to use it.\n- **Source profile:** SCEEUS expert analysis drawing partly on official statistics and Golos; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 election mechanism.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Kremlin/CEC system; personal Putin endorsement is independently documented by E029.\n- **Contrary:** Authorities presented electronic voting as convenient and modern.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.6, 4B.9, 4B.10; 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.6.\n\n## E027 — Arrest and exclusion of independent election observers\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://sceeus.se/en/publications/russias-2023-september-elections-a-dress-rehearsal-for-vladimir-putin/#observers|Golos-leader-detained-observation-restricted`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Third, despite maintaining…”\n- **Claim:** Golos co-chair Grigory Melkonyants was detained three weeks before the election. Political parties and candidates could no longer send representatives to election commissions as observers, while freelance journalists and candidate proxies were barred from polling stations.\n- **Source profile:** SCEEUS analysis linked to BBC and Golos; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Federal/Kremlin system; no evidence Putin personally ordered Melkonyants’s arrest.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.5, 3B.8; 4B.2, 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.9, 4B.10; 7B.6, 7B.7.\n\n## E028 — Reported fraud and coercion in 2023 regional elections\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://sceeus.se/en/publications/russias-2023-september-elections-a-dress-rehearsal-for-vladimir-putin/#violations|Golos-reported-2023-election-fraud`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “Fourth, according to Golos…”\n- **Claim:** Golos reported carousel voting, ballot stuffing, voter coercion, and invalid voter-register entries, describing the elections as the worst in 23 years.\n- **Source profile:** SCEEUS relaying domestic observer findings; `medium_high`, with source-chain limitation.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Regime/election-administration pattern; personal Putin direction not established.\n- **Contrary:** The analysis acknowledges occasional opposition breakthroughs and difficulty exercising total local control.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.5, 4B.10.\n\n## E029 — Putin endorsed expanded remote electronic voting\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ecfr.eu/article/russias-regional-elections-a-prelude-to-putins-future/?amp#manipulation|Putin-endorsed-remote-electronic-voting`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://ecfr.eu/article/russias-regional-elections-a-prelude-to-putins-future/?amp\n- **Title:** “Russia’s regional elections: A prelude to Putin’s future”\n- **Publisher:** European Council on Foreign Relations\n- **Date:** 13 September 2023\n- **Locator:** “Manipulation at play,” bullet concerning remote electronic voting.\n- **Claim:** Remote electronic voting expanded to 25 regions with Putin’s endorsement, particularly in areas expected to be challenging.\n- **Source profile:** European policy think tank; `medium_high`; links a Kremlin source.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Direct Putin endorsement, though the fetched page rather than the Kremlin transcript supplies the claim.\n- **Contrary:** ECFR notes the Kremlin could not guarantee every local victory.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.9, 4B.10; 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.6.\n\n## E030 — Opposition candidates excluded through repression and exile\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://ecfr.eu/article/russias-regional-elections-a-prelude-to-putins-future/?amp#opposition|opposition-candidates-exile-detention`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** First bullet under “Manipulation at play.”\n- **Claim:** ECFR assessed that formal competition had declined and that many opposition candidates could not participate because they were in exile or detention.\n- **Source profile:** ECFR; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 regional elections.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Kremlin regime context; no individual Putin order shown.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.5; 4B.1–4B.3, 4B.5, 4B.10.\n\n## E031 — Golos account of censored campaigning and observer restrictions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://transparenciaelectoral.org/en/transparencia/golos-there-will-be-no-competition-in-these-local-elections-in-russia/#no-competition|Golos-campaign-observer-restrictions`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://transparenciaelectoral.org/en/transparencia/golos-there-will-be-no-competition-in-these-local-elections-in-russia/\n- **Title:** “GOLOS: there will be no competition in these local elections in Russia”\n- **Publisher:** Transparencia Electoral, reporting an interview with Golos co-chair Stanislav Andreichuk\n- **Date:** September 2023\n- **Locator:** “No competition” section.\n- **Claim:** Andreichuk described campaigning as heavily restricted and censored, said the CEC had barred freelance journalists from polling stations, and stated that harsh participation conditions caused opposition candidates to withdraw or avoid running.\n- **Source profile:** Election-transparency NGO relaying a domestic monitor; `medium_high`, but not a direct Golos report.\n- **Period fit:** Immediately before the 2023 elections.\n- **Ruler attribution:** System/CEC, not personal Putin direction.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1–4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9; 3B.5, 3B.8.\n\n---\n\n## E032 — Russia’s 2023 growth was substantial but war-spending driven\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.voanews.com/a/russia-economy-grew-in-2023-despite-war-and-sanctions/7478952.html#opening|Russia-2023-growth-war-spending`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://www.voanews.com/a/russia-economy-grew-in-2023-despite-war-and-sanctions/7478952.html\n- **Title:** “Russia’s Economy Grew in 2023, Despite War and Sanctions”\n- **Publisher:** Voice of America\n- **Date:** 8 February 2024\n- **Locator:** Opening and “War spending stimulates economy.”\n- **Claim:** Rosstat reported 3.6 percent GDP growth in 2023 after contraction in 2022. Independent economists cited by VOA attributed much of the rebound to major fiscal stimulus and defense expenditure.\n- **Source profile:** Reputable public-service media citing Rosstat, IMF, RAND, and named experts; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Retrospective report directly about 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Government war-spending strategy under Putin; GDP growth itself cannot be attributed solely to him.\n- **Contrary/mitigating points:** Demonstrates resilience and real growth despite sanctions; sustainability and composition were questioned.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.9, 5B.10; 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.10.\n\n## E033 — Inflation and central-bank response\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.voanews.com/a/russia-economy-grew-in-2023-despite-war-and-sanctions/7478952.html#inflation|Russia-2023-inflation-interest-rate`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “One side effect…”\n- **Claim:** Inflation was 7.4 percent in 2023, down from 11.9 percent in 2022. The central bank raised interest rates to approximately 16 percent amid inflationary pressure from fiscal stimulus.\n- **Source profile:** VOA citing Russian data and named experts; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 outcome and response.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Inflation was linked to government fiscal expansion, while monetary correction was led by the central bank, whose independent contribution must not be credited automatically to Putin.\n- **Contrary:** Inflation improved compared with 2022, although it remained high.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.3, 5B.7, 5B.9, 5B.10; 8B.8 as context for institutional adaptation.\n\n## E034 — Sanctions adaptation and urban labor conditions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.voanews.com/a/russia-economy-grew-in-2023-despite-war-and-sanctions/7478952.html#sanctions-adaptation|Russia-trade-China-low-unemployment`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** “West considers new sanctions” and “Life continues ‘as usual’.”\n- **Claim:** Russia replaced much lost European trade with increased China trade, while unemployment remained near historic lows and wage increases offset some inflation, especially in large cities.\n- **Source profile:** VOA with named expert attribution; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** 2023 conditions reported in early 2024.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Government adaptation and private/state trading responses contributed; causal credit should be limited.\n- **Contrary/limitations:** Conditions were worse outside major metropolitan areas, with shortages and supply concentration favoring large cities.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.4, 5B.7–5B.10; 6B.3, 6B.10; 8B.7–8B.10.\n\n## E035 — Fiscal sustainability concerns\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.voanews.com/a/russia-economy-grew-in-2023-despite-war-and-sanctions/7478952.html#fiscal|Russia-2023-borrowing-wealth-fund-deficit`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs describing total spending, financing, and deficit.\n- **Claim:** Russia financed elevated expenditure through taxes, National Wealth Fund drawdowns, and borrowing. VOA reported a government deficit near 10 percent of the overall budget and quoted experts questioning sustainability.\n- **Source profile:** VOA/RAND expert analysis; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 fiscal record.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Government fiscal strategy under Putin, though precise budget design involves cabinet and finance institutions.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.9, 5B.10; 8B.7, 8B.10.\n\n---\n\n## E036 — War spending outpaced health and education\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#wartime-priorities|Russia-2023-defense-versus-services`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **URL:** https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education\n- **Title:** “The crisis affecting Russia’s public services: healthcare, education, and the postal service”\n- **Publisher:** Centre for Eastern Studies / OSW\n- **Date:** 27 June 2024\n- **Locator:** “Wartime priorities and unfulfilled promises,” first two paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** OSW estimated that defense and internal-security expenditure increased nearly 70 percent to 10 trillion rubles in 2023, while health and education funding increased about 20 percent. It assessed that the war and military prioritization intensified chronic public-service problems.\n- **Source profile:** Government-funded foreign-policy research institute; `medium_high`; uses Russian official and independent sources.\n- **Period fit:** Retrospective analysis of 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Broad Kremlin budget priority; direct Putin linkage is reinforced by E008.\n- **Contrary:** Public-service spending still increased nominally rather than falling across the board.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.6, 5B.8–5B.10; 6B.1, 6B.2, 6B.4, 6B.9, 6B.10; 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.6.\n\n## E037 — Limited implementation of Putin’s public-sector salary instructions\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#salary-targets|Putin-salary-order-implementation-failure`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs stating that only 11 regions fully implemented recommendations and discussing Putin’s end-2023 instruction.\n- **Claim:** By the end of 2023, only 11 regions had fully implemented all public-administration salary recommendations. Putin had ordered implementation by year-end and earmarked federal funding, but OSW identified unrealistic assumptions, weak enforcement, insufficient funding, regional debt, and war-spending priority as obstacles.\n- **Source profile:** OSW; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 implementation and outcome.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Strong for the directive and prioritization; implementation was divided among federal, regional, and municipal bodies.\n- **Contrary:** The government provided subsidies and targeted funds, showing some remedial action.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.2, 5B.6–5B.10; 6B.1, 6B.2, 6B.4, 6B.5, 6B.9, 6B.10; 8B.2, 8B.5–8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n## E038 — Healthcare staffing and compensation problems\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#healthcare-staff|Russia-2023-healthcare-staff-pay`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** “Problems faced by the healthcare sector,” opening paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** In 2023, 75 percent of healthcare workers reportedly earned below the May-decree targets; nearly 60 percent of physicians said salaries did not meet basic needs, and almost 80 percent worked two or more jobs.\n- **Source profile:** OSW synthesis using Russian sources; `medium_high`, but exact percentages should retain OSW’s source-chain limitation.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Links to Putin’s May-decree targets and federal/regional implementation; not solely presidential responsibility.\n- **Contrary:** Special monthly allowances were introduced in 2023 and later raised.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2–6B.6, 6B.9, 6B.10; 5B.6, 5B.8.\n\n## E039 — Vaccine, disease, and medicine-access deterioration\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#disease-medicines|Russia-2023-vaccine-medicine-shortages`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Healthcare paragraphs covering measles, whooping cough, influenza, medicine availability, insulin, and HIV treatment.\n- **Claim:** OSW reported sharp 2023 rises in measles, whooping cough, and influenza amid vaccine shortages and low coverage. The range of medicines available in pharmacies fell 11 percent; shortages affected insulin and HIV treatment.\n- **Source profile:** OSW synthesis; `medium_high`; reported figures come from multiple Russian official and independent sources and should not be treated as a single primary dataset.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** National policy and war/sanctions context; direct Putin causal responsibility is partial and mediated.\n- **Contrary:** Pharmaceuticals were not formally sanctioned, and domestic generics replaced some imported products.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.10; 5B.6, 5B.9.\n\n## E040 — Teacher shortages and regional inequality\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#education-staff|Russia-2023-teacher-shortage`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** “Problems faced by the education sector,” first three paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** Russia had an estimated shortage of 11,000 teachers in 2023. Teacher departures reached 193,500, and shortages and low pay varied sharply by region, increasing workloads and forcing teachers to cover multiple subjects.\n- **Source profile:** OSW synthesis; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Federal/regional service system; Putin’s wage directives and national priorities are relevant but not exclusive causes.\n- **Contrary:** The government operated a rural-teacher incentive program and a school-renovation program.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2–6B.5, 6B.9, 6B.10; 5B.6, 5B.8.\n\n## E041 — Education militarization and politicization\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#education-ideology|Russia-2023-school-militarization`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** Education paragraph discussing “Conversations about Important Things,” military training, veterans, and school naming.\n- **Claim:** OSW reported increasing ideological and military content in education, including obligatory patriotic lessons, restored basic military training, employment of former service members, and naming schools after participants in the war.\n- **Source profile:** OSW; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** 2023/broader wartime school policy.\n- **Ruler attribution:** National Kremlin program; exact presidential order for each component was not retrieved.\n- **Contrary:** Some measures can be framed officially as civic or defense education; evidence concerns politicization and service effects rather than physical safety.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.5, 4B.6; 6B.1, 6B.4, 6B.7–6B.9; 8B.1–8B.3, 8B.6.\n\n## E042 — Regional exclusion and postal-service deterioration\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#postal|Russia-2023-postal-pension-delays`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** “Problems faced by Russian Post.”\n- **Claim:** Staffing and financial problems closed or reduced rural post-office services in 2023, with reported pension-payment delays in several regions. OSW assessed that these problems disproportionately affected rural residents and pensioners.\n- **Source profile:** OSW; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Exact 2023 examples.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Federal company and ministry policy; direct Putin attribution is weak.\n- **Contrary:** The government considered reallocating resources to preserve remote offices and later approved modernization instructions.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.7–6B.10; 5B.6, 5B.8.\n\n## E043 — Public services remained operational despite chronic crisis\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/osw-commentary/2024-06-27/crisis-affecting-russias-public-services-healthcare-education#functional-crisis|Russia-services-no-total-collapse`\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Locator:** “A functional crisis,” final three paragraphs.\n- **Claim:** OSW concluded that Russian public services were in chronic crisis but were unlikely to collapse completely. Federal authorities used provisional remedies and multiyear programs that slowed deterioration and maintained services at a relatively low level.\n- **Source profile:** OSW; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Retrospective assessment substantially grounded in 2023.\n- **Ruler attribution:** System-level response under Putin; not proof of personal operational management.\n- **Contrary role:** Meaningful mitigation against interpreting shortages as service collapse.\n- **Mappings:** 6B.2, 6B.4–6B.6, 6B.9, 6B.10; 8B.5–8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n## E044 — Broader inherited human-development context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/fr/document/EPRS_BRI(2022)729375#summary|Russia-human-development-baseline`\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n- **URL:** https://www.europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/fr/document/EPRS_BRI(2022)729375\n- **Title:** “Human development in Putin’s Russia: What the data tell us”\n- **Publisher:** European Parliamentary Research Service\n- **Date:** 21 April 2022\n- **Locator:** Page abstract.\n- **Claim:** Russia retained very-high-HDI status, free public healthcare, and relatively inclusive education, while inequality, ill-equipped hospitals, and deteriorating post-2014 living conditions complicated that baseline.\n- **Source profile:** Legislative research service; `medium_high`.\n- **Period fit:** Inherited baseline immediately preceding 2023, not target-year evidence.\n- **Ruler attribution:** Broader Putin-period conditions; individual outcomes remain multi-causal.\n- **Contrary role:** Records substantial inherited social infrastructure and high aggregate human-development status.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.8, 5B.10; 6B.2, 6B.8, 6B.10.\n\n---\n\n# Chapter continuation outcomes\n\n## 1B\n\n### New yield\n\nE016–E020 add:\n\n- CTBT reversal/testing signaling;\n- tactical nuclear deployment to Belarus;\n- Moscow’s retained control over use;\n- external intergovernmental reaction;\n- independent technical context and mitigating qualifications.\n\n### Source diversity\n\n- Arms Control Association\n- SIPRI\n- Current Time with linked Kremlin source\n- EEAS/EU at the UN\n- NTI\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\nStill not well covered:\n\n- internal command-and-control safety;\n- 2023 hotline/crisis-communication use;\n- expert appointments;\n- biological, cyber, and AI catastrophic-risk policy;\n- independently verified weapon numbers in Belarus.\n\nThe chapter is materially improved but not fully saturated. Further searches would likely produce diminishing returns unless primary Kremlin/CTBTO documents and crisis-channel records are available.\n\n## 2B\n\n### New yield\n\n- E012–E015 split the former omnibus UN item.\n- E021 adds independent OHCHR support on POW/civilian torture.\n- E022 adds a specific accountability and amnesty finding.\n- E023 adds the Black Sea Grain Initiative withdrawal and resulting attacks.\n- E024 records exact UN report scope.\n\n### Improved gaps\n\n- prisoner treatment;\n- accountability/remedy;\n- one concrete 2023 diplomatic/food-security agreement reversal;\n- separate civilian-harm findings.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\nSearches did not recover a reliable, precise 2023 source reconstructing:\n\n- comprehensive ceasefire offers;\n- alternatives Putin personally considered and rejected;\n- a primary 2023 Putin statement setting all war aims;\n- incident-level command responsibility;\n- meaningful de-escalatory actions.\n\nSearch results were polluted by 2025–2026 peace-talk coverage. The absence of a retained 2023 negotiation item remains a research gap, not proof that no contacts occurred.\n\n## 3B\n\n### New yield\n\nE027, E030, and E031 add:\n\n- detention of an election monitor;\n- restrictions on observation;\n- repression-driven opposition exclusion;\n- censored campaign conditions.\n\nE038–E042 add vulnerable-group and regional welfare context but should not be misclassified as physical-safety evidence unless a direct safety nexus is established.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\nNot materially improved:\n\n- Putin’s own inciting or dehumanizing rhetoric;\n- Chechnya-specific 2023 physical-integrity cases;\n- reliable aggregate political-prisoner count;\n- discipline of abusive domestic officials;\n- direct target-year LGBT/minority violence protection.\n\nThese need dedicated UN Special Rapporteur, Memorial/OVD-Info, SOVA, and regional source retrieval.\n\n## 4B\n\n### New yield\n\nE025–E031 provide granular regional-election evidence from three additional organizations:\n\n- SCEEUS\n- ECFR\n- Transparencia Electoral/Golos\n\nCovered themes now include:\n\n- regional-election competitiveness;\n- electronic voting;\n- direct Putin endorsement of remote electronic voting;\n- observer detention;\n- observer exclusion;\n- candidate exclusion;\n- reported fraud and coercion;\n- limited contrary evidence of local opposition success.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\n- primary statutory text for observation restrictions;\n- detailed candidate-registration case list;\n- gender and minority political inclusion;\n- meaningful institutional-restraint evidence;\n- term-limit or succession action specifically occurring in 2023.\n\nThe chapter now has substantially better source diversity and event granularity.\n\n## 5B\n\n### New yield\n\nE032–E037 add:\n\n- realized 2023 GDP growth;\n- war-spending composition;\n- inflation and interest-rate response;\n- sanctions adaptation;\n- low unemployment and wage offsets;\n- regional shortages;\n- fiscal financing and sustainability concerns;\n- incomplete implementation of public-sector salary directives.\n\n### Attribution safeguards\n\n- Central-bank correction is not automatically Putin’s achievement.\n- Growth resulted partly from war fiscal stimulus and cannot be treated as broad household prosperity.\n- Trade substitution involved government, firms, China, and private adaptation.\n- Regional disparities qualify national averages.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\n- independently measured poverty and real household-income series with precise locators;\n- property-rights and competition outcomes;\n- firm-level import-substitution quality;\n- business-investment and productivity evidence;\n- direct Putin economic personnel choices.\n\nThe chapter now exceeds the minimum evidence threshold and has meaningful positive and adverse evidence, but distributional research remains incomplete.\n\n## 6B\n\n### New yield\n\nE036–E044 add:\n\n- realized public-service resource tradeoffs;\n- public-sector salary implementation;\n- health-worker shortages and compensation;\n- medicine, vaccine, insulin, and HIV-treatment access;\n- teacher shortages and regional inequality;\n- education politicization;\n- rural postal and pension access;\n- evidence that services continued operating despite crisis;\n- inherited high-HDI and inclusive-system context.\n\n### Contrary evidence preserved\n\n- Health and education spending increased nominally in 2023.\n- Authorities introduced medical allowances and rural incentive programs.\n- Services did not collapse.\n- Russia inherited extensive public-health and education infrastructure.\n- Putin reportedly resisted the hardest anti-abortion proposals, although no primary transcript was fetched.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\n- authoritative 2023 life expectancy, mortality, poverty, and pension-adequacy data;\n- national housing/sanitation and food-security outcomes;\n- systematic evidence about mobilized soldiers’ families;\n- direct causal evaluation of Putin-specific welfare programs.\n\nThe chapter has moved from one policy signal to a multi-domain evidence base, though much evidence comes from one OSW report and should retain source-concentration caution.\n\n## 7B\n\n### New yield\n\nE027 adds a specific scrutiny-retaliation nexus: detention of a leading independent election observer shortly before elections.\n\nE037 adds a documented gap between a Putin directive and implementation, relevant to promises and correction but not proof of dishonesty.\n\nE009/E010 remain useful for loyalty, incomplete reporting, and patronage patterns.\n\n### Gaps that could not be responsibly improved\n\nDirect searches did not recover a precise, target-year:\n\n- beneficial-ownership record;\n- personal or family asset finding;\n- court or audit judgment establishing enrichment;\n- asset declaration;\n- documented material falsehood followed by correction or refusal to correct;\n- inquiry response concerning Putin’s own finances.\n\nSearches mostly surfaced older Navalny allegations, post-2023 retrospectives, social-media/video material, or secondary summaries without traceable financial documents. None was promoted into final evidence.\n\nThe personal-integrity attribution gap remains severe.\n\n## 8B\n\n### New yield\n\nCross-chapter additions now broaden the governing portfolio:\n\n- **Nuclear/international influence:** E016–E020\n- **War and external policy:** E021–E023\n- **Electoral/regime control:** E025–E031\n- **Economic adaptation:** E032–E035\n- **Public-service implementation:** E036–E043\n\n### Implementation signals\n\n- successful budgetary mobilization for war;\n- continued electoral control and expanded electronic voting;\n- sanctions/trade adaptation and low unemployment;\n- services maintained despite chronic stress.\n\n### Failure or fragility signals\n\n- inflation and fiscal sustainability pressure;\n- regional inequality;\n- incomplete implementation of Putin’s salary instructions;\n- health and education staffing deficits;\n- continuing reliance on provisional remedies;\n- Wagner command failure from the pre-existing ledger.\n\n### Attribution safeguards\n\n- outcomes are shared with central bank, cabinet, regions, firms, and external trade partners;\n- electoral manipulation is regime-level unless direct Putin endorsement is documented;\n- OSW’s public-service findings show mixed implementation rather than simple collapse;\n- E011 remains retrospective context.\n\nThe portfolio is broader but still weighted toward war, regime control, and crisis adaptation. Non-security policy durability remains less documented.\n\n---\n\n# Rejected and blocked continuation candidates\n\nAdd these manifest entries:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R010\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report-on-the-status-of-tactical-nonstrategic-nuclear-weapons-negotiations/|unusable-extraction\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Fetch returned embedded image/base64 without a reliable precise text locator; search snippet was not promoted.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R011\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.aa.com.tr/ru/[putin-belarus-iskander]|fetch-protocol-error\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Fetch failed with multiple Transfer-Encoding headers; underlying article was unavailable.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R012\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.tnsr.org/2023/03/the-role-of-u-s-diplomacy-in-countering-russias-nuclear-threats-and-misbehavior/|404\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Candidate URL returned HTTP 404.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R013\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.ebrd.com/documents/comms-and-bis/regional-economic-prospects-may-2023.pdf|raw-pdf-no-locator\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"PDF fetch returned raw binary without recoverable page text or a precise Russia locator.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R014\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://2021-2025.state.gov/the-wagner-groups-atrocities-in-africa-lies-and-truth/|unusable-extraction\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Fetch returned embedded image/base64 without locatable substantive text.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R015\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"search:Russia-2023-peace-negotiations|future-result-pollution\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Results were dominated by 2025–2026 negotiations or generic retrospectives and did not yield a precise target-year source.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"provisional_id\": \"R016\",\n    \"canonical_fact_key\": \"search:Putin-2023-beneficial-ownership|no-target-year-record\",\n    \"disposition\": \"rejected\",\n    \"reason\": \"Candidates were older allegations, post-period retrospectives, videos, social posts, or lacked underlying financial/legal records.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n# Manifest update instructions\n\nThe persisted manifest should:\n\n1. Change **E002** from `final_evidence` to `context`, adding a reason such as:\n   - `\"reason\": \"Superseded by deterministic source-claim splits E012-E015; retained only for accounting continuity.\"`\n2. Append E012–E044 with dispositions shown above.\n3. Append R010–R016.\n4. Preserve all previous entries and stable IDs.\n\n## New-item disposition summary\n\n- **Final evidence:** E012–E019, E021–E023, E025–E043\n- **Context:** E020, E024, E044\n- **Rejected:** R010–R016\n- **Changed:** E002 from final evidence to context because it was split\n\n---\n\n# Round 1 global audit\n\n## Material improvements\n\n- **1B:** moved from one main event/source family to New START, CTBT, Belarus deployment, retained-control, and contrary technical analysis.\n- **2B:** UN omnibus evidence was split; POW treatment, accountability, and grain-agreement withdrawal were added.\n- **4B:** regional-election coverage now has event-level support from multiple independent organizations.\n- **5B:** realized macroeconomic outcomes, inflation, sanctions adaptation, fiscal pressure, and regional distribution entered the ledger.\n- **6B:** multiple service domains and implementation outcomes now exist.\n- **8B:** governing portfolio expanded beyond Wagner and budget inputs.\n\n## Persistent high-risk gap\n\n**7B remains the clearest unresolved attribution problem.** The available country corruption, patronage, and scrutiny-suppression evidence still does not establish a target-year personal financial or deception record for Putin.\n\n## Temporal-fit audit\n\n- E016–E019, E021–E023, E025–E043 directly concern 2023 or report retrospectively on 2023.\n- E020 is later-maintained context.\n- E024 is an index/report-scope item.\n- E044 is inherited 2022 context.\n- Post-period analysis was retained only where it directly documents 2023 facts, and its retrospective nature is stated.\n\n## Source-concentration audit\n\n- 6B now has many claim units but considerable concentration in OSW; its distinct locators should be preserved, and source concentration reported rather than hidden.\n- 4B diversity materially improved.\n- 1B now has several source families.\n- 5B has VOA, OSW, SIPRI, local WDI/BTI context, and existing FIIA evidence.\n- 7B remains thin despite continued searching.\n\n## Attribution audit\n\nStrong direct Putin attribution now exists for:\n\n- New START suspension;\n- Belarus tactical-nuclear deployment announcement;\n- CTBT-related signaling;\n- electronic-voting endorsement;\n- war-budget signature;\n- public-sector salary directives.\n\nState/institutional rather than personal attribution remains necessary for:\n\n- battlefield and detention abuses;\n- observer detention;\n- election fraud;\n- inflation correction by the central bank;\n- regional service implementation;\n- firm-level sanctions adaptation;\n- individual corruption or enrichment allegations."}}
